# B Boutique — environment variables
#
# Names only. Never commit a value to this file: it is checked into the
# repository and read by everyone with access to it.
#
# Copy to .env.local for local development, and set the same names in the
# Vercel project for preview and production.

# ── Indexing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "true" turns search-engine indexing ON. Anything else (including unset)
# leaves the site noindex, which is the deliberate default while the site
# still carries placeholder testimonials and unconfirmed shop policy.
# Read at build time, so changing it needs a redeploy — which is correct,
# because going live IS a deploy. See src/app/layout.tsx.
ALLOW_INDEXING=

# ── Contact form ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three are required together. Until every one is set, POST /api/contact
# answers 503 and the form tells the visitor plainly that the message was NOT
# sent, and gives them the shop's phone number instead. It never reports
# success without a send. This is a LAUNCH BLOCKER, by design.
#
# CONTACT_TO    the shop's inbox — where enquiries actually arrive.
#               Nobody has supplied one yet. Do not guess an address.
# CONTACT_FROM  the verified sender, on a domain verified with the provider
#               (e.g. "B Boutique <hello@yourdomain.co.uk>"). Never the
#               visitor's own address: sending as them fails SPF/DKIM for
#               their domain and lands the shop's mail in spam. Their address
#               goes in reply_to instead.
# RESEND_API_KEY  the provider API key. Server-only — it is read inside a
#               route handler and must never be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_.
CONTACT_TO=
CONTACT_FROM=
RESEND_API_KEY=

# ── The shop ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three are required together. Until every one is set, POST /api/checkout
# answers 503 and the bag page tells the customer plainly that checkout could
# not be started and that nothing has been charged. It never claims an order
# was placed. This is a LAUNCH BLOCKER, by design.
#
# SUMUP_API_KEY        a SumUp API key. Server-only — it is read inside a
#                      route handler and must never be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# SUMUP_MERCHANT_CODE  the merchant code the payment belongs to.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL the site's own origin: https://bboutiqueclee.com
#                      SumUp needs somewhere to send the customer back to and
#                      that URL has to be absolute. It is the checkout's
#                      `redirect_url` — NOT `return_url`, which is a server
#                      callback. See the SumUp section of CLAUDE.md.
#
#                      SETTING THIS TURNS THE CHECKOUT ON. It is the last of
#                      the three variables /api/checkout waits for, and the
#                      other two are already set in the Vercel project. The
#                      moment it exists, the shop can take real money at the
#                      prices in lib/catalogue.ts — every one of which is
#                      invented. Do not set it until her own prices are in.
#
# There is no fourth variable for a webhook signing secret, and there is not
# going to be one: SumUp publishes no payment webhook. The success page
# confirms a payment by asking, with SUMUP_API_KEY, rather than by being told.
SUMUP_API_KEY=
SUMUP_MERCHANT_CODE=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ── The stock list ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both are required together. Until they exist, /stock shows a plainly worded
# "not set up yet" page and lets NOBODY in — a page that defaults to open when
# its configuration is missing is the wrong way round.
#
# DATABASE_URL      injected by the Neon Postgres integration on Vercel, and
#                   CONFIRMED PRESENT 2026-09-20 on Production and Preview.
#                   It is Neon's POOLED string, which is what a serverless
#                   function wants: one that holds a TCP connection exhausts
#                   the pool the first time two people load the shop at once.
#
#                   Neon injects sixteen variables. The full set, so nobody
#                   wonders later which one is which:
#                     DATABASE_URL              <- pooled. This is the one.
#                     DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED     direct, no pgbouncer
#                     POSTGRES_URL              read as a fallback
#                     POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING
#                     POSTGRES_URL_NO_SSL
#                     POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL
#                     POSTGRES_HOST / _USER / _PASSWORD / _DATABASE
#                     PGHOST / PGHOST_UNPOOLED / PGUSER / PGPASSWORD /
#                     PGDATABASE
#                     NEON_PROJECT_ID
#                   Nothing reads the rest; they are Neon's, not ours.
#
#                   NO CUSTOM PREFIX was set when connecting the database. A
#                   prefix would have renamed these to STORAGE_URL and the
#                   like, and /stock would have reported "no database" while a
#                   working one sat next to it.
#
#                   The stock tables create themselves on first use.
# STOCK_PASSPHRASE  the passcode the shop types into /stock. Eight characters
#                   minimum. Server-only — never prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_. It
#                   protects stock counts, not money: it cannot reach a
#                   payment, a card or an order's payment details.
DATABASE_URL=
STOCK_PASSPHRASE=
